# Security

## Secrets Management

- Never hardcode secrets, API keys, tokens, or passwords
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Never commit .env files, credentials.json, or key files
- Rotate secrets if accidentally exposed

## Input Handling

- Validate all user input at the boundary
- Use parameterized queries (never string concatenation for SQL)
- Sanitize data before rendering (prevent XSS)
- Validate file uploads (type, size, content)

## Dependencies

- Keep dependencies updated
- Review changelogs before major version bumps
- Pin dependency versions in production
- Audit dependencies for known vulnerabilities

## OWASP Top 10

Follow OWASP top 10 guidelines:
1. Broken Access Control — enforce authorization at every layer
2. Cryptographic Failures — use standard libraries, not custom crypto
3. Injection — parameterize all queries
4. Insecure Design — threat model before building
5. Security Misconfiguration — secure defaults, no debug in production
6. Vulnerable Components — keep dependencies patched
7. Authentication Failures — use proven auth libraries
8. Data Integrity Failures — verify signatures and checksums
9. Logging Failures — log security events, never log secrets
10. SSRF — validate and restrict outbound requests

## Network

- Use HTTPS/TLS for all external communication
- Validate certificates
- Use least-privilege for service accounts and IAM roles
